                        <note>
                            <date type="foundation" when="1327">The 
                                local tradition tells that the church was founded in 1327 and associates the foundation with 
                                <persName ref="PRS1854Amdase">King ʿAmda Ṣǝyon I </persName></date>.
                            <ab type="history">Dabr.</ab>
                            <ab type="tabot">
                                <measure quantity="4"/> tābots, dedicated to                            
                                <persName ref="PRS10775Trinity" type="tabot">the Holy Trinity</persName>, 
                                <persName ref="PRS4377Gabriel" type="tabot">Archangel Gabriel</persName>, 
                                <persName ref="PRS5684JesusCh" type="tabot">Saviour of the World</persName>, 
                                the main being of <persName ref="PRS4585Georgeo" type="tabot">St George of Lydda</persName>
                                The feasts of St George are celebrated on 18 Ṭǝrr, 23 Yakātit, 23 Miyāziyā 23, and 7 Ḥamle, and 7 Ḫǝdār. 
                                
                            </ab>                        
                            
                            <ab type="description">22 monks, 5 nuns, 13 priest, 15 deacons, 22 other servants;  
                                three teachers of <foreign xml:lang="gez">ʾaqqʷāqqʷām</foreign> (litugrical dance), three teachers of 
                                <ref type="work" corresp="LIT3178Deggwa">Dǝggʷā</ref>,
                                two teachers of <foreign xml:lang="gez">qǝddāse</foreign> (liturgy),
                                two teachers of <foreign xml:lang="gez">qǝne-poetry</foreign>,
                                four of <foreign xml:lang="gez">tǝrgʷāme</foreign> (interpretation) and 
                                one of <foreign xml:lang="gez">zǝmmāre</foreign> (chanting)
                                (the stand for <date>August 2026</date>).
                                
                                The subsequent development of the church is associated with the Gondarine time, 
                                particularly with the reign of King <persName ref="PRS5616IyasuI">ʾIyāsu I (r. 1682–1706)</persName>.
                                The present church was built in its current form and inaugurated on 17 June 1966, 
                                during the reign of Emperor <persName ref="PRS5226haylase">Ḫāyla Śǝllāse I</persName>.
                                It was formally inaugurated as a cathedral by the
                                patriarch ʾabuna Mātiyās on <date>25 February 2014</date>.
                                The church formerly had an ancient museum located approximately 40 meters north. 
                                The old museum of the church is a historic building, approximately five meters high, that is
                                traditionally associated with the Gondarine period. 
                                Local tradition connects the building with the Portuguese 
                                and the reign of <persName ref="PRS5616IyasuI">ʾIyāsu I ʾAdyām Sagad</persName>.
                                The former museum building has since been replaced by a new museum
                                which houses a collection of ecclesiastical and royal objects, 
                                including royal garments, ceremonial robes, 
                                crowns, chalices, hand crosses, 
                                embroidered vestments, ceremonial umbrellas, and parchment manuscripts.
                                49 manuscripts were inventarized in <date>August 2026</date> in the <foreign xml:lang="am">ʿǝqā bet</foreign> 
                                by Tibebu Anteneh and Mihreteab Merachew Worku. 
                                </ab>
                            
